/*
===========================================================
        NEGATIVE MARKING / VISITED METHOD
===========================================================

Use this method when:

1. Array values are in the range 1 to n.
2. We want to find duplicate/repeating elements
   or sometimes missing elements.

Example:

    arr = {1, 3, 4, 2, 2}

Here n = 5

-----------------------------------------------------------
1. IMPORTANT MAPPING
-----------------------------------------------------------

C++ array index starts from 0.

So we map:

    Value 1  -> index 0
    Value 2  -> index 1
    Value 3  -> index 2
    Value 4  -> index 3
    Value 5  -> index 4

Therefore:

    value -> index

    index = value - 1

That's why we use:

    arr[index - 1]

-----------------------------------------------------------
2. WHY abs()?
-----------------------------------------------------------

During this method, we change some numbers to negative.

Example:

    5 becomes -5

But we still need to know that the original value was 5.

So:

    abs(5)  = 5
    abs(-5) = 5

Therefore:

    int index = abs(arr[i]);

means:

    "Give me the original positive value."

-----------------------------------------------------------
3. MAIN IDEA
-----------------------------------------------------------

We use NEGATIVE and POSITIVE signs as a VISITED marker.

    Positive  = not visited yet
    Negative  = already visited

We don't need a separate visited[] array.

-----------------------------------------------------------
4. THE MAIN CODE
-----------------------------------------------------------

    int index = abs(arr[i]);

    if(arr[index - 1] < 0) {

        // Already negative
        // It means this value was already visited
        // Therefore this value is repeating/duplicate

    }
    else {

        // First time seeing this value
        // Mark its position as visited
        arr[index - 1] *= -1;
    }

-----------------------------------------------------------
5. EXAMPLE
-----------------------------------------------------------

Array:

    {1, 3, 4, 2, 2}

Start:

    1   3   4   2   2

-----------------------------------------------------------
i = 0

arr[i] = 1

index = abs(1)
index = 1

index - 1 = 0

Check:

    arr[0]

It is positive.

So mark it negative:

    arr[0] *= -1

Array becomes:

   -1   3   4   2   2

Meaning:

    "1 has been visited."

-----------------------------------------------------------
i = 1

arr[i] = 3

index = abs(3)
index = 3

index - 1 = 2

Check:

    arr[2]

It is positive.

Mark it negative:

    -1   3  -4   2   2

Meaning:

    "3 has been visited."

-----------------------------------------------------------
i = 2

arr[i] = 4

index = abs(4)
index = 4

index - 1 = 3

Check:

    arr[3]

It is positive.

Mark it:

    -1   3  -4  -2   2

Meaning:

    "4 has been visited."

-----------------------------------------------------------
i = 3

arr[i] = 2

index = abs(2)
index = 2

index - 1 = 1

Check:

    arr[1]

It is positive.

Mark it:

    -1  -3  -4  -2   2

Meaning:

    "2 has been visited."

-----------------------------------------------------------
i = 4

arr[i] = 2

index = abs(2)
index = 2

index - 1 = 1

Check:

    arr[1]

But arr[1] is already negative:

    -3

Therefore:

    2 has already been visited.

So:

    2 is the duplicate/repeating element.

-----------------------------------------------------------
6. EASY WAY TO REMEMBER
-----------------------------------------------------------

Think like this:

        VALUE
          |
          ↓
      abs(value)
          |
          ↓
      value - 1
          |
          ↓
       INDEX
          |
          ↓
    Check the sign
          |
       ┌──┴──┐
       ↓     ↓
   Positive Negative
       ↓     ↓
    First     Already
    visit     visited
       ↓         ↓
    Make       Duplicate /
   negative    Repeating

-----------------------------------------------------------
7. CODE FOR FINDING ONE DUPLICATE
-----------------------------------------------------------

    vector<int> arr{1, 3, 4, 2, 2};

    for(int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {

        int index = abs(arr[i]);

        if(arr[index - 1] < 0) {

            cout << "Duplicate = " << index << endl;
            break;

        }
        else {

            arr[index - 1] *= -1;

        }
    }

-----------------------------------------------------------
8. CODE FOR FINDING ALL REPEATING ELEMENTS
-----------------------------------------------------------

    vector<int> arr{1, 3, 4, 2, 2, 3};

    for(int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {

        int index = abs(arr[i]);

        if(arr[index - 1] < 0) {

            cout << "Repeating element = "
                 << index << endl;

        }
        else {

            arr[index - 1] *= -1;

        }
    }

Difference:

    ONE duplicate:
        use break

    ALL repeating elements:
        don't use break

-----------------------------------------------------------
9. MOST IMPORTANT RULE
-----------------------------------------------------------

This method is mainly used when:

    Values are in the range 1 to n.

For example:

    {1, 3, 5, 3, 4}     ✅

But:

    {3, 4, 5, 7, 9}     ❌

if n = 5,

because 7 and 9 cannot be converted into valid
indexes using this method.

-----------------------------------------------------------
10. ONE LINE TO REMEMBER
-----------------------------------------------------------

    "The value tells me which position to visit."

    "Positive = first visit."

    "Negative = already visited."

===========================================================
*/